  <owner-decision date="2026-09-18">ISS-023: "When access is renewed the user should have the same level as previous access period." Resolved as D-7 option 1 — PIN WHAT EXISTS, no contract change. AccessLevel stays non-nullable; the renewal-versus-set distinction already exists as two ROUTES, and the rule already holds by construction on the renewal route. ISS-028: "Fix." Move the conferral check ahead of the write, so a request that will be refused changes nothing.</owner-decision>

  <prompt>Two register entries on one endpoint, deliberately one task.

ISS-023. The read path composes effective access as GroupBy(root).Max(level) over active, unexpired rows (ExternalParticipationService.cs:945-950 and :993-1000). The /grant write path writes the REQUESTED level to the elected survivor (GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:248-255, the UpdateAsync at :260) and then deactivates every other row (CollapseDuplicatesAsync, :459-486), so a re-grant at ViewOnly over a key holding a FullAccess duplicate lowers effective access — on a request whose caller may have meant only to renew. Under D-7 option 1 the answer is NOT to change what /grant writes. The distinction already exists as two routes: set-record-share-expiry writes only sprk_expiresdate and no level (SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:213-216; the file contains no reference to AccessLevel or sprk_accesslevel at all), so the owner's rule already holds there. What is missing is that nothing NAMES the rule, and /grant's own explicit-level-change behaviour is only half tested. So this half of the task is a pinning job, not a behaviour change.

ISS-028. On the same endpoint, in the same region, the write happens BEFORE the refusal. :248 computes levelChanged; :250-260 writes sprk_accesslevel = requestedLevel; only then, at :306-319, does the ADR-003 conferral check fire and return the outcome that the caller sees as 409 sdap.grant.expired_not_restored (emitted at :137-145, titled "Grant did not take effect"). The 409's own message tells the caller the request did not take effect — and the expired row's level has already been changed. It bites only when the request carries no new expiry AND a different level, because a supplied expiry resolves the conferral question first (:281-282). It is pre-existing; task 106 changed the survivor election, not this ordering. It is unpinned: GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:812-825, the one test aimed at this path, sends the SAME level, so levelChanged is false, the write is skipped, and the defect is invisible to it.

Fix the ordering, and pin it with a test that can actually see it.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — C#/.NET, the external grant lifecycle and its characterization suite.</role>
  <goal>A /grant request that will be refused mutates nothing. The renewal route's "no level is written" property is named and pinned rather than merely true. An explicit level change on /grant still applies exactly as requested, in BOTH directions. No request shape, status code or reasonCode changes.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Verified at HEAD on 2026-09-19 while authoring (see notes for the full premise record). The conferral check is movable because it depends on nothing the write produces: effectiveExpiry = ExternalGrantLifecycle.EffectiveExpiry(request.ExpiryDate, survivor.ExpiresDate, today) (:306) and both helpers are pure — EffectiveExpiry returns explicitExpiry ?? rowExpiry ?? DefaultExpiry(today) (ExternalGrantLifecycle.cs:187-188) and ConfersAccessOn(expiresDate, today) returns expiresDate is null || expiresDate.Value greater-or-equal today (ExternalParticipationService.cs:124-125). All three inputs are available before the write, so hoisting the check above the update block is behaviour-preserving on the success path.

Two things the register states that source contradicts, both already corrected in this POML. (1) CollapseDuplicatesAsync does NOT write the level — it only deactivates non-survivors via DeactivateAsync (:459-486); the level write is the update block at :250-260. The effect the register describes still holds, by two mechanisms rather than one. (2) task 063's share-user path is NOT a sprk_accesslevel write and cannot be broken by anything this task does inside CreateGrantAsync: it writes a Dataverse POA share through IDataverseRecordShareService (GrantAccessAsync/ModifyAccessAsync at InternalShareEndpoints.cs:344-346) with a rights mask from RecordShareLevels, and its own class doc states at :26 that "nothing here reads or writes sprk_externalrecordaccess". The downgrade that this task must not break is therefore /grant's OWN explicit downgrade — which has no test today: GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:325 covers ViewOnly to FullAccess (an upgrade) only.

Also already true, and the reason the ISS-023 half is small: the renewal route's payload is already asserted to carry the expiry and nothing else, at RecordShareExpiryTests.cs:132-133 — OnlyContain(u =&gt; u.fields.Count == 1 andalso u.fields.ContainsKey("sprk_expiresdate")), with the message "the change writes the expiry and nothing else — level, grantee and state stay as they were". That count-based assertion is strong (a level would make it 2) but it does not NAME the owner's rule and does not cover the lapsed-row case explicitly. Strengthen and name it; do not rebuild it.

Contract facts behind D-7 option 1: GrantAccessRequest.cs:39 is a non-nullable ExternalAccessLevel beside a nullable DateOnly? ExpiryDate at :40, and :22-25 documents ExpiryDate as "Optional in the REQUEST" while :21 carries no such clause; InviteExternalUserRequest.cs:29 is likewise a non-nullable int AccessLevel beside DateOnly? ExpiryDate at :32. The enum has no zero member (ExternalCallerContext.cs:314-319: 100000000 / 100000001 / 100000002), so an omitted level binds to 0 and is refused. Making the level omissible is option 2, which the owner did NOT choose.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs (hoist the :306-319 conferral check above the :250-260 update block; correct the :296-302 comment)</file>
      <file role="modify">tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs (the ISS-028 pin at/beside :812-825; the missing /grant downgrade guard beside :325)</file>
      <file role="modify">tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/RecordShareExpiryTests.cs (name and strengthen the no-level pin at :132-133)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs (the renewal route — :213-216 writes only sprk_expiresdate; READ, do not change)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalGrantLifecycle.cs (EffectiveExpiry :187-188, ElectSurvivor, ConferralRank — pure, request-independent)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs (ConfersAccessOn :124-125; the read path's GroupBy/Max at :945-950 and :993-1000)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/InternalShareEndpoints.cs (share-user is a POA share, NOT a grant-row level write — see :26 and :344-346)</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-18 (D-7 option 1)">Scope: contract. No request-shape change. AccessLevel stays non-nullable on both request records; no new field, no new route, no changed meaning for an existing one. The renewal-versus-set distinction IS the two existing routes. Making the level omissible (option 2) was considered and NOT chosen.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-18 / ISS-023">Scope: level semantics on /grant. 🔴 Do NOT implement the rule as max(requested, existing), and do not infer "renewal" from row state. Max makes downgrades impossible; row-state inference is clock-dependent (the same payload is a set at 23:59Z and a renew at 00:01Z) and, because reminders fire 30/14/7/3/1 days BEFORE expiry, the ordinary renewal arrives on a still-live key and would be classified "set" anyway — leaving ISS-023 unfixed in the common case. An explicit level change on /grant must still apply exactly as requested, in EITHER direction.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-18 / ISS-028">Scope: ordering. The ADR-003 conferral check moves AHEAD of the write, so a request that will be refused mutates nothing. Two non-fixes are forbidden: a compensating write (write the level, then restore it after refusing) leaves a real mutation and a real failure window; and suppressing or softening the 409 removes the true statement the caller depends on. The refusal stays exactly as it is — only the write moves.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: fail-closed. Fail closed on every path that cannot be evaluated. "Do not report success over a grant that confers nothing" now also means "do not mutate the row you are about to refuse". The check must remain a statement about the WHOLE key (it reads the elected survivor, and an expired survivor proves every active row is expired — task 106).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-019">Scope: response shape. ProblemDetails unchanged. The 409 keeps its status, title ("Grant did not take effect"), detail text, reasonCode sdap.grant.expired_not_restored, traceId and accessRecordId exactly as emitted at :137-145. A changed status code or reasonCode is an escalation, not an implementation choice.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038 / review-2026-08-24">Scope: verification. Test-first is mandatory here, not stylistic: the new ISS-028 pin MUST be written against unmodified production code and MUST be observed to FAIL before the reorder lands. The defect survived precisely because the one test aimed at this path could not see it; a pin that has never failed has not been shown to be able to. Pair positives with negatives; perturbation is mandatory. Tests stay at the existing KEEP path (tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/), no new mock-boundary shapes.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR. BFF-touching: state the Placement Justification explicitly (it is "in BFF, existing endpoint, no new surface"); verify publish size against a FRESH worktree of origin/master rather than the recorded baseline, publish and zip both sides from SHORT paths with the same tool, and sanity-check that the file counts match; no new HIGH-severity CVE; tests updated per the test obligation.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11">Scope: surface. Modification only. Add no new service, endpoint, DTO, request field, DI registration or package. Reuse the existing pure helpers (EffectiveExpiry, ConfersAccessOn) and the existing FakeGrantTable observables (LevelUpdateCount, ExpiryUpdateCount, ActiveRows) rather than adding new test scaffolding.</constraint>
    <constraint source="audit 2026-09-18">Scope: boundaries. Do NOT edit task 099, TASK-INDEX beyond this task's own row, or notes/defer-issues.md. Task 099 needs its own level constraint (owner: non-negotiable) but that is 099's amendment, recorded in this task's notes so it is not lost. Task 101 needs nothing — its column list already includes access level.</constraint>
  </constraints>

