  <steps mode="prescriptive">
    <step order="0">Re-verify every premise at HEAD before touching anything: the update block and its line span, the conferral check and its line span, the 409 emission site, the renewal route's payload, and the existing tests at GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:325 and :812-825 and RecordShareExpiryTests.cs:132-133. Line numbers in this POML were true on 2026-09-19 and will drift. Record any premise that has changed, and re-read the two register entries before deciding anything.</step>
    <step order="1">RED FIRST, against unmodified production code. Write the ISS-028 pin — an expired survivor, no new expiry, a DIFFERENT requested level — and assert the stored level is unchanged after the refusal. Run it and OBSERVE IT FAIL. Do not proceed until it has failed for the right reason (the level was written), not for a setup reason. This ordering is the task's one binding sequence: the defect survived because the test aimed at it could not see it.</step>
    <step order="2">Now hoist the conferral check above the update block in CreateGrantAsync, leaving the refusal itself byte-identical. Re-run: the new pin goes green, and the existing same-level test at :812-825 and the with-new-expiry test at :835 stay green. Correct the :296-302 comment so it no longer vouches for an ordering that was right for expiry and wrong for level.</step>
    <step order="3">Close the ISS-023 half: add the missing /grant explicit-DOWNGRADE guard beside :325 (FullAccess to ViewOnly on a live row lowers the row), and name plus strengthen the renewal route's no-level pin in RecordShareExpiryTests so it asserts the absence of an sprk_accesslevel key explicitly, including for an already-lapsed row that is renewed.</step>
    <step order="4">Perturbations: restore the original order and confirm at least one test fails; replace the reorder with a compensating write-then-restore and confirm at least one test fails; seed a max(requested, existing) level rule and confirm the new downgrade guard fails.</step>
    <step order="5">Build, the two full test classes, the full suite, publish size against a fresh master worktree per the §10 procedure, CVE check. Write notes/task-113-renewal-preserves-level.md; update this task's TASK-INDEX row and current-task.md.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The renewal-versus-explicit-change distinction is a CONTRACT decision, and D-7 option 1 asserts it is already expressed as two routes. If verification at HEAD contradicts that — a shipped caller that renews through /grant rather than set-record-share-expiry, or a renewal surface that cannot reach the renewal route — then the owner's rule is NOT expressible without a request-shape change. STOP and escalate with options (the owner's own option 2, a nullable AccessLevel, is on file). Do NOT infer renewal intent from the payload or from row state.</trigger>
    <trigger>If hoisting the conferral check would change ANY observable of the response — status code, title, detail text, reasonCode, accessRecordId, or which requests are refused at all — STOP and escalate. Option 1 explicitly excluded contract change, so a behavioural difference beyond "the refused request no longer mutates the row" is out of scope for this task, not a judgment call to make inside it.</trigger>
  </escalation>

