  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A refused /grant request — expired survivor, no new expiry, a DIFFERENT requested level — leaves sprk_accesslevel UNCHANGED on the row, and no update carrying sprk_accesslevel is issued at all.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">That pin was observed to FAIL against unmodified production code before the reorder landed, and the failure is recorded in the task notes. A pin that has never failed does not satisfy this criterion.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The refusal itself is unchanged: same 409, same title, same detail text, same reasonCode sdap.grant.expired_not_restored, same traceId and accessRecordId extensions. The existing same-level test at GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:812-825 still passes, unmodified in meaning.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative — the check still does not fire when it should not: a request WITH a new expiry over an expired row still succeeds, still writes the requested level, and still returns success (the :281-282 property).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative — an explicit level change on /grant still applies in BOTH directions: the existing upgrade test (:325, ViewOnly to FullAccess) stays green, and a new guard proves an explicit FullAccess-to-ViewOnly re-grant on a live row does lower the stored level.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The renewal route still writes NO level: set-record-share-expiry's payload is asserted to contain no sprk_accesslevel key (not merely a field count), for a live share and for an already-lapsed share that is renewed, and the stored level is unchanged afterwards.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No contract change: AccessLevel remains non-nullable on both request records, no request field is added or removed, no new route, and no status code or reasonCode is added, removed or changed on any /grant path.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: restoring the write-before-check order fails at least one test; a compensating write-then-restore fails at least one test; a max(requested, existing) level rule fails the downgrade guard.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The :296-302 comment no longer vouches for an ordering it was wrong about for level, and says which property the ordering does and does not carry.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; both full test classes green; full suite green; publish size reported as both absolute sizes plus the delta against a fresh origin/master worktree with matching file counts, and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH-severity CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in task notes.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Register: notes/defer-issues.md § ISS-023 and § ISS-028. Owner brief: notes/owner-decision-brief-2026-09-18.md § D-7 (lines 357-417) and § ISS-028 (lines 516-535). Related: tasks 010, 023, 063, 097, 098, 099, 101, 106.

TWO ISSUES, ONE TASK. ISS-028 (#1008) is folded in here on the owner's recommendation — same endpoint, same region, and this task already opens the file. Closing task 113 closes BOTH #1002 and #1008. Close them together or not at all: the ISS-023 half is a pinning job whose value is mostly that it names a rule, and the ISS-028 half is the actual defect.

⚠️ TASK 099 MUST SEPARATELY GAIN THE LEVEL CONSTRAINT (owner: non-negotiable) — and it is 099's amendment, NOT this task's scope. Do not edit 099 from here. As specified, 099's acceptance criteria never mention level, so it would present a date picker while the server changed the level. The owner's exact text, preserved so it is not lost: the Expiration picker is the only renewal control and MUST NOT change any level — a picker change calls only set-record-share-expiry (test: zero /grant calls, body carries no accessLevel); after refresh every Current Access level badge is unchanged; a "+ Contact" or "+ Organization" re-add of a principal already listed is an explicit SET and MUST send the picked level. Task 101 needs nothing; its column list already includes access level (101 POML :34).

STEPS MODE — why prescriptive. The mode binds the task's step sequence, and here exactly one sequence is load-bearing: the pin must be written and observed to FAIL before the production change. That is not process decoration, it is the same shape as the defect being fixed. ISS-028 survived because GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:812-825 — the one test aimed at this path — sends the same level, so levelChanged is false and the defect is invisible to it. An executor who reorders first and then writes the pin gets a green test and no evidence it can see anything, which reproduces the original failure exactly. Directional mode would leave that sequence adaptable, so it is wrong for this task. The rest of the sequence is genuinely coarse and the constraints, not the step order, carry the semantics.

NO justification element, deliberately. This task modifies existing files only: one reorder inside an existing method, one comment correction, and tests for existing surface. Per CLAUDE.md §11 that is explicitly outside the justification rule, and the validator only requires the element when a relevant-file carries role="new". A hollow justification would fail review, so none is offered — this note records that as a decision, not an omission.

PREMISE RECORD — verified against source at HEAD on 2026-09-19 while authoring. Every load-bearing claim below was read, not inferred. Premise rot is this project's documented failure mode — notes/task-accuracy-audit-2026-09-18.md:9 records 19 prior instances of a task's factual claims drifting out of step with the code — and two of this task's own inherited premises were wrong, corrected above.
- TRUE. Update block: GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:248 levelChanged; :254-255 sets sprk_accesslevel = requestedLevel in the payload; :260 UpdateAsync. Conferral check: :306 effectiveExpiry, :307 ConfersAccessOn, :315-319 the refusal outcome. So the write does precede the refusal.
- TRUE. The 409 is emitted by the caller at :137-145: status 409, title "Grant did not take effect", reasonCode sdap.grant.expired_not_restored, plus traceId and accessRecordId.
- TRUE, exact lines. The supplied-expiry note is at :281-282. Task 106's comment block is :296-302, and the phrase "the ADR-003 ordering below ... are load-bearing and true" is at :301-302.
- TRUE. GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:812-825 calls Request(expiryDate: null); Request's level parameter defaults to ViewOnly (:233) and the row is seeded ViewOnly (:816), so levelChanged is false and the write is skipped. The defect is invisible to it.
- TRUE, and this is the feasibility premise for the whole fix: the check depends on nothing the write produces. EffectiveExpiry (ExternalGrantLifecycle.cs:187-188) and ConfersAccessOn (ExternalParticipationService.cs:124-125) are pure, and all three inputs exist before the write.
- TRUE. Renewal route writes only the date: SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:213-216, and a grep of that entire file for AccessLevel or sprk_accesslevel returns NO matches. Its doc at :29-31 confirms lapsed rows are renewed too (owner 2026-09-11).
- TRUE. D-7's contract facts: GrantAccessRequest.cs:39-40 (non-nullable level beside nullable ExpiryDate) and :21-25 (only ExpiryDate is documented optional); InviteExternalUserRequest.cs:29 and :32; enum ExternalCallerContext.cs:314-319 has no zero member.
- FALSE as written, corrected. The register says "the write path's CollapseDuplicatesAsync writes the requested level to the survivor". It does not: CollapseDuplicatesAsync (:459-486) only deactivates non-survivors via DeactivateAsync. The level write is the update block at :250-260. The EFFECT the register describes still holds — via two mechanisms rather than one, and for either election outcome — so ISS-023 stands; only the mechanism attribution was wrong.
- FALSE as framed, corrected. "Task 063's share-user path exists to downgrade" is true of the feature but NOT of this code path: share-user writes a Dataverse POA share through IDataverseRecordShareService (InternalShareEndpoints.cs:344 GrantAccessAsync / :346 ModifyAccessAsync) using a RecordShareLevels rights mask, and its class doc states at :26 that "nothing here reads or writes sprk_externalrecordaccess". It is a different store and cannot be broken by a change inside CreateGrantAsync. The downgrade actually at risk from a max() rule is /grant's own, and that one has NO test today — :325 covers the upgrade direction only. Hence the new downgrade guard in step 3, which is the real forcing function the constraint needs.
- ALREADY SATISFIED, in part. The renewal route's no-level property is already pinned implicitly at RecordShareExpiryTests.cs:132-133, which asserts fields.Count == 1 and ContainsKey("sprk_expiresdate") with the message "the change writes the expiry and nothing else — level, grantee and state stay as they were". Strong, but unnamed and count-based, and the lapsed-row case (:141) does not assert it. Strengthen and name; do not rebuild.</notes>

